AcquireLock's non-blocking flock (LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB) fits its one existing use, the per-torrent spawn lock: contention there means another process already legitimately owns this torrent's lock, and the correct response is to give up immediately (surface process.ErrLockHeld), not wait around. The upcoming port-registry use (internal/portpool, landing in a later commit) has the opposite shape of contention: another process doing a brief read-check-claim-write on the shared ports.json is expected and normal, and the correct response there is to wait a moment for it to finish, not fail.

Rather than growing AcquireLock a blocking bool/flag parameter to cover both, this refactors the shared open-file/flock/release logic into a private acquireLock(path, flags syscall.LockType) helper, with AcquireLock now just acquireLock(path, syscall.LOCK_EX|syscall.LOCK_NB) and the new AcquireLockBlocking just acquireLock(path, syscall.LOCK_EX). A single parameterized function would have made every call site's intent -- 'give up on contention' vs. 'wait out contention' -- something the reader has to trace into the argument rather than read directly off which function is called. AcquireLockBlocking is unused as of this commit; internal/portpool is what actually calls it, in a later commit.
